<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>8.9.3: Writing an Equation to Represent a Quadratic Function with Only One Solution</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02235</md:content-id>
    <md:title>8.9.3: Writing an Equation to Represent a Quadratic Function with Only One Solution</md:title>
    <md:uuid>e0ac33bf-8f4f-434a-86d4-0725ef17cc79</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity </title>
<para>In previous lessons, we saw that a <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">quadratic equation</term> can have zero, one, or two solutions.</para>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Sketch graphs that represent three <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">quadratic functions</term>: one that has no zeros, one with one zero, and one with two zeros.</para>
    <para>Write down your answers, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answers:</para>
  <para>No zeros:</para>
  <para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/a655a0de355fddfd470e6ea05905772ddb33755f"/></media></figure></para>
  <para>One zero:</para>
  <para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/34231239a973fd94fc82fc36965512282698e375"/></media></figure></para>
  <para>Two zeros:</para>
  <para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/a77acf9fb1fd35c6277cd1175717ddf23bb9765b"/></media></figure></para>
</solution></exercise>
</example>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Graph the function defined by \(f(x) = x^2− 2x + 1\). Examine the \(x\)-intercepts of the graph.</item>
</list>
<para>Use the Desmos graphing tool or technology outside the course.</para>

<div class="os-raise-ib-desmos-gc" data-schema-version="1.0"/>

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Explain what the \(x\)-intercepts reveal about the function.</para>
  </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your explanation.</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> The graph only intersects the \(x\)-axis at one point: \((1, 0)\). The function only has one zero.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Solve \(x^2− 2x + 1 = 0\) by using the factored form and <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">zero product property</term>. What solutions do you get?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the solutions.</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> Rewrite the standard form to \((x − 1)(x − 1) = 0\). The solutions are \(x=1\) and \(x=1\). Because the two values are the same, we say that this equation has one solution and that \(x = 1\) is a double root for the equation.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>Try to find another quadratic function that you think will only have one zero.</item>
</list>
<para>Use the Desmos graphing tool or technology outside the course.</para>

<div class="os-raise-ib-desmos-gc" data-schema-version="1.0"/>

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Graph it to check your prediction, then enter your final answer.</para>
  </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer.</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> For example: \(f(x) = (x + 4)(x + 4)\). When graphed, this function only has one zero, at \(x=-4\).</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

